{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>When you lift up the head.</b> It should have said merely 'when you count' — and what is written, ״וְנָתְנוּ אִישׁ כֹּפֶר נַפְשׁוֹ״ — the word ״אִישׁ״ is superfluous, for it should have said ״וְנָתְנוּ כֹּפֶר נַפְשָׁם״. From this it is understood that each one gives individually, for afterward it says ״כֹּל הָעֹבֵר עַל הַפְּקֻדִים״ etc. And further, it is difficult according to the opinion of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, who said (Yerushalmi Shekalim, chapter 2, halakhah 3) that this atonement was to atone for the sin of the Calf — if so, why should they give the ransom specifically at the time of the counting? And further it is difficult: for in the sin of the Calf all Israel were equal, elders together with youths, yet the ransom was given only by one who is from twenty years old and up." "And it appears possible to settle all this, by means of what is said (Exodus 32:34): ״וּבְיוֹם פָּקְדִי וּפָקַדְתִּי עֲלֵיהֶם אֶת חַטָּאתָם״. What is ״בְּיוֹם פָּקְדִי״? Rather, this teaches that the iniquity of the Calf was not forgiven them except on account of the sinners being many — for many, mercy increases — for the Holy One, blessed be He, is especially compassionate toward the many, as written (Job 36:5): ״הֵן אֵל כַּבִּיר לֹא יִמְאָס״, and as written (Jonah 4:11): ״וַאֲנִי לֹא אָחוּס עַל נִינְוֵה אֲשֶׁר יֶשׁ בָּהּ הַרְבֵּה מִשְׁתֵּים עֶשְׂרֵה רִבּוֹא״ etc. And so the Gemara concludes (Avodah Zarah 4b): Rabbi Yohanan said in the name of Rabbi Shimon ben Yohai: 'David was not worthy of that deed, and Israel were not worthy of that deed; why did they do it? Only in order to provide an opening of the mouth for penitents.' And both are needed, for if it were taught only about the many, I would say it is because many, mercy increases, etc. And according to this, this atonement serves only at a time when each person sits within his people, as the Shunammite woman said (II Kings 4:13): ״בְּתוֹךְ עַמִּי אָנֹכִי יוֹשָׁבֶת״. But at a time when they are counted one by one, there is reason to fear that the counting will cause each person alone, on his own, to have his remembrance brought on high, and his ledger examined with every merit and liability he has — and perhaps his merits will not outweigh his liabilities; and for this reason the iniquity of the Calf, which was already forgiven, will be recalled to him, for it was forgiven only to the many and not to individuals, and the counting causes each individual to be remembered on his own. Therefore it says ״וּבְיוֹם פָּקְדִי״, and this is an expression of counting, and its meaning is: when they are counted for some need, then the remembrance of each particular individual will rise before Me, and then ״וּפָקַדְתִּי עֲלֵיהֶם אֶת חַטָּאתָם״ — for the sin returns and awakens. Therefore He commanded here concerning this ransom to atone for the sin of the Calf at the time of the counting, for the station of each and every one will be reckoned. Therefore our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Avot 2:4): ״אַל תִּפְרֹשׁ מִן הַצִּבּוּר״. And consequently those younger than twenty, who were not included in this counting, always sat within their people. And therefore it says ״כִּי תִשָּׂא אֶת רֹאשׁ״ — for since the remembrance of every head comes before HaShem, blessed be He, on its own, therefore ״וְנָתְנוּ אִישׁ״ — every particular individual for whom there is reason to fear that his ledger may be examined, and he may be sought and found to have had a share in that disgraceful act, therefore at that time he shall give the ransom of his soul." "And this fits well also according to those who say that this ransom was to save from the evil eye that holds sway at the time of the counting — for at that time there is opportunity for the supernal princes of the nations to indict and say: 'What distinction and greatness is there for Israel in this number above all the nations, when these are idol worshippers and those are idol worshippers?' Therefore at that time they need a ransom to quiet the attribute of judgment. And through this is resolved what is asked here: 'But how many nations count by the head and are not harmed?' For it is not any counting that causes it, but rather this counting that comes to take them and to lift them up in ״כִּי תִשָּׂא אֶת רֹאשׁ״ (Exodus 30:12); therefore there is reason to fear from the zealots who assail them." "And what they ask — why Saul and David transgressed and counted them without half-shekels — it appears possible to answer that the evil eye holds sway only in a counting that is something novel. By way of parable: if one sees a hundred gold coins in the hand of a poor man, he needs to be far more careful of the evil eye than when one sees thousands in the hand of a rich man. So Israel, when they went out of Egypt, were held to be few in number, for the affliction of Egypt had diminished them, and suddenly the number rose to six hundred thousand. If so, then certainly there is reason to fear the evil eye. But afterward, when they came to a settled land and Judah and Israel were as the sand of the sea (I Kings 4:20), there was nothing novel in a large people being counted at a time of need." "<b>And they shall give, every man, the ransom of his soul.</b> In parashat Terumah, three contributions are mentioned, and in all of them the expression of taking is used, whereas in this parashah the expression of giving is used in all of them. And what is it that our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Menahot 29a, Tosafot s.v. שלשה) — that Moses was challenged by these shekels — what was the challenge? It appears to me that the expression of taking implies that one takes from each person against his will, and Moses was challenged by this matter, to coerce each person to give, for they are a stiff-necked people. And to rectify this, it is written in parashat Bemidbar (Numbers 1:3–4): ״תִּפְקְדוּ אֹתָם לְצִבְאֹתָם אַתָּה וְאַהֲרֹן, וְאִתְּכֶם יִהְיוּ אִישׁ אִישׁ לַמַּטֶּה, אִישׁ רֹאשׁ לְבֵית אֲבֹתָיו הוּא״. And one should consider why He commanded to bring the head of every tribe to the counting — rather, it is surely so that they would not harbor suspicions against Moses, saying that he takes the bekha per head for his own needs; for when each person sees that the head of his tribe stands there and oversees this, then they will give of good will, for each one will think that the head of his tribe would not allow any wrong to be done to him. And this is what it says: ״כִּי תִשָּׂא אֶת רֹאשׁ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — its meaning is: when you lift up the head of every tribe to the counting — this is what it says ״לִפְקֻדֵיהֶם״ — then ״וְנָתְנוּ אִישׁ כֹּפֶר נַפְשׁוֹ״ — presumably they will give of good will and you will not need to coerce them. And this entire verse would be a response to what Moses was challenged by, and the Holy One, blessed be He, answered him: ״כִּי תִשָּׂא״ etc. — its meaning is: when you do this thing, then ״וְנָתְנוּ״ — they will give of themselves. Therefore ״רֹאשׁ״ is said in the singular, because it speaks of the head of every single tribe." "And as for what Rashi explained — that the three offerings mentioned here [are distinct], one of them being a voluntary donation — I do not know which of them speaks of that voluntary donation, for if you examine the language of the verse carefully you will find that all of them speak of a half-shekel per head. And there is a further difficulty: for in parashat Terumah (Exodus 25) he attributed the first two offerings to HaShem, blessed be He, for in the first it says ״וְיִקְחוּ לִי״ (Exodus 25:2) — 'for My name,' and in the second it says ״תִּקְחוּ אֶת תְּרוּמָתִי״ (Exodus 25:2). But in the third it says ״וְזֹאת הַתְּרוּמָה אֲשֶׁר תִּקְחוּ מֵאִתָּם״ (Exodus 25:3), for it depends on the willingness of the giver. Yet here he called all three ״תְּרוּמַת ה׳״ (Exodus 30:13)." "Therefore it seems to me more correct to say that all three speak of a half-shekel per head, for we find that Moses counted them three times: once here, a second time in the book of Numbers, a third time in the plains of Moab. And all of them were by means of half-shekels, which are an obligation and an atonement for the soul. Therefore he attributed all three to HaShem, blessed be He, for two reasons: first, because HaShem, blessed be He, has a claim of merit in them, for all souls belong to HaShem, blessed be He, and in His compassion for His creatures He takes a half-shekel in exchange for the soul; therefore all three are called ״תְּרוּמַת ה׳.״ Second, because in every place where there is an occasion for arrogance, where the rich man has room to boast over the poor man and say to him, 'My donation is greater than yours,' there the Holy One, blessed be He, does not call His name, blessed be He, upon it. Therefore He did not attribute to HaShem, blessed be He, the third donation in parashat Terumah. But here, since all three were the half-shekel, in which rich and poor are equal and there is no room for one to boast over the other, therefore all three are called ״תְּרוּמַת ה׳.״ And the reason He did not mention in parashat Terumah except two that were a half-shekel per head, is because there He mentioned only the things that were an immediate need: for the first offering there was an immediate need to make the sockets from it, and from the second, public offerings, and the third was a donation for the need of the Tabernacle and its vessels. But here the commandment came to Moses that every time he would count them he should count them by means of the shekels — therefore He needed to mention three, to inform that these half-shekels are needed only for the three countings of Moses, but not for all generations. And Saul and David prove that they did not count them by means of the shekels. And therefore it does not say here ״שְׂאוּ אֶת רֹאשׁ,״ but rather ״כִּי תִשָּׂא,״ to tell you: specifically at the time of ״כִּי תִשָּׂא״ — when you lift — and not for all generations." "Another thing: since the three offerings mentioned in parashat Terumah all use the language of taking (קִיחָה), which implies that they would take from them by force, therefore the special name was not mentioned in any one of them, for this is called ״מִצְוַת אֲנָשִׁים״ and not the commandment of HaShem, blessed be He — for when a man forces another to give, the offering is called by the name of that man, as it is written (Isaiah 29:13) ״יַעַן כִּי נִגַּשׁ הָעָם הַזֶּה״ — meaning that they are pressed and coerced regarding every matter of commandment; concerning this it says ״וַתְּהִי יִרְאָתָם אֹתִי מִצְוַת אֲנָשִׁים״ — for it is as though they were commanded by the men who coerce them. But here, concerning all three, it says ״וְנָתְנוּ,״ which speaks of a time when they give from good will without any coercion; therefore all three are called תְּרוּמוֹת ה׳, for this giving is surely for the sake of Heaven. And even though the name was not mentioned in parashat Terumah in all of them, nevertheless He mentioned in the first ״לִי״ and in the second ״תְּרוּמָתִי,״ for HaShem, blessed be He, has a claim of merit in them in any case — and this is a precious interpretation."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {5 "The antecedent of 'he' (קרא) in the final sentence appears to be Scripture or Moses; rendered as 'he' to preserve the ambiguous Hebrew subject."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:25:02.683414849Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}